FLOATING POINT: math.l and math881.l====================================Microware ships two C math libraries, and the runtime modules that matchthem are in CMDS:    -l=/dd/LIB/math.l      software floating point.  Runs on any 68k.    -l=/dd/LIB/math881.l   uses a 68881/68882 coprocessor.  Needs one.They give the same answers.  Measured with math.l, the software library,against the true values:                        math.l              true value    sqrt(2)             1.4142135624        1.4142135624    exp(1)              2.7182818285        2.7182818285    sin(1)              0.8414709848        0.8414709848    pow(2,10)           1024.0000000000     1024    H(100)              5.1873775176        5.1873775176(H(100) is the sum of 1/k for k = 1 to 100, which adds up a hundred smallerrors if there are any.)Nearly everything on this disk that uses floating point was built withmath.l, so it runs on a 68k with or without a coprocessor.  math881.l isfaster on a machine that has one.  paranoia and savage measurefloating-point accuracy and are worth running on your own system.AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FILE WAS WRONG-----------------------------------------It said math.l was roughly single precision: exp(1) 2.7182831246,sin(1) 0.8414711041, pow(2,10) 1024.0039.  Those figures came from os9exec,not from the library.  os9exec builds before 2026-09-14 did not set the68000's X flag after NEG, and math.l's double-precision addition depends onit.  A real 68k was never affected.  If a program shows figures like thoseabove, the emulator needs updating.